In this exercise we calculate the final throughtput of a simple arithmetic triad
for each element of three arrays a,b,c
	a[i] = b[i] + s*c[i]
being s a double. The sizes of the arrays vary from 1KB to 512MB. One can be tempted
to say that the throughtput is exactly the same as for in the past case, on which
there were no difference on the array size, since the prefetcher is constantly 
bringing cache lines to the L1. However the problem is different, since now, the 
prefetcher has to bring lines from the a,b,c arrays, and there is a limit on how
many petitions can be flying from one level on the memory hierarchy and the next one.
So we will still see the jumps one the total working set exceeds the sizes of the cache.

Here it is the result:
# bytes_per_array  working_set_bytes  GB/s_code  GB/s_with_write_allocate
        1024          3072	52.373      69.830
        2048          6144	55.150      73.533
        4096         12288	57.026      76.035
        8192         24576	57.978      77.304
       16384         49152	43.051      57.401
       32768         98304	42.856      57.141
       65536        196608	43.120      57.493
      131072        393216	42.935      57.247
      262144        786432	41.116      54.821
      524288	   1572864	28.510      38.013
     1048576	   3145728	26.303      35.071
     2097152	   6291456	26.265      35.020
     4194304	  12582912	25.743      34.324
     8388608	  25165824	20.731      27.642
    16777216	  50331648	17.698      23.597
    33554432     100663296	16.490      21.987
    67108864     201326592	16.110      21.481
   134217728     402653184	15.964      21.286
   268435456     805306368	15.924      21.232
   536870912    1610612736	15.912      21.216

Remembering that the cache sizes are
L1d cache:             32K
L1i cache:             32K
L2 cache:              1024K
L3 cache:              25344K

We can see how there are clear steps when we exceed the size of a memory level.
It is measured in GB/s because this is not a problem on how fast the FPU can operate
on the data (which for this case is almost in one cycle) but instead how much memory
can be provided to the program, in this case the processor is most of the time waiting
for the data than really operating on it. This is called a memory bound program.

